Explanation/derivation:
- In each step t the algorithm:
  1) performs Poisson subsampling with rate q = L/N (i.e., includes each example independently with probability q);
  2) computes per-example gradients, clips them to have L2 norm at most C, and sums them;
  3) adds Gaussian noise Z_t ~ N(0, σ^2 C^2 I_d) to the sum.

  This is exactly the subsampled Gaussian mechanism (SGM) under the add/remove adjacency, with sensitivity at most C for the sum. The accountant O is assumed to calibrate σ so that T compositions of this SGM meet the target (ε, δ) under add/remove neighbors.

- The subsequent division by |L| and the SGD update are data-independent post-processing of the privatized sum; by the post‑processing property of DP, they do not degrade privacy.

- Therefore, provided PoissonSample implements Bernoulli/Poisson subsampling (each record included independently with probability q, i.e., no multiple copies of the same record in a batch), the algorithm is (ε, δ)-DP for the ε, δ used to obtain σ from O. If instead “PoissonSample” were sampling with replacement (allowing multiple inclusions of the same record in one step), the per-step sensitivity would be unbounded and the accountant’s calibration would no longer apply.

